#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Enforces the layering contract between models, presenters, components and
# views. The boundaries are otherwise convention only, so they erode silently:
# a raw model reaches a template, a view calls `policy(...)` itself, a presenter
# news up its own collaborators, and nothing fails until someone notices by eye.
#
#   R1  Views hold only presenters.
#       Mailers count: ActionMailer::Base and ActionController::Base both
#       descend from AbstractController::Base, a mailer assigns ivars and its
#       template reads them, so a mailer view is a view. Mailers have no
#       viewer and no Pundit, so no policy is injected — but they still hand
#       the template presenters rather than models.
#       Every instance variable a view touches must be a presenter. Not a model,
#       not a component, not a primitive, not a collection of models. Assigning a
#       controller-built component would satisfy "no raw model in the ERB" while
#       moving the model into the controller — presenters-only closes that.
#
#   R2  Policies are injected, never looked up.
#       A view never calls `policy(...)`; a presenter or component never calls
#       `SomePolicy.new`. Policy instances arrive in the presenter's options hash
#       as `policy:`. A capability rename then changes one construction point
#       instead of being chased through templates.
#
#   R3  Presenters take (subject, options).
#       One positional subject — a model, a presenter, or an array of either —
#       plus an options hash. No second positional, no loose keywords, no
#       url_helpers, no view context. This is the rule that `page_action(game:,
#       can_manage:, is_participant:, membership:, urls:)` violated.
#
#   R4  Components take presenters, components, arrays of components, and
#       hashes. Never a raw model — a component holding a model computes what the
#       presenter should have.
#
# Classification is by INHERITANCE, not by name. Every class under app/ is
# indexed with its superclass, and a constant is resolved by walking that chain
# to a known root (ApplicationRecord / BasePresenter / ApplicationComponent).
# A name-based check would confuse `SceneSummary` (model) with
# `SceneSummaryPresenter`, would not know what `ScenePageAction` is, and would
# be fooled by a `class FooPresenter` that never inherits BasePresenter — named
# like a presenter, but not one.
#
# R1 reads the `T.let` type declarations that `# typed: strict` forces onto
# controller instance variables; a controller that is not yet strict cannot be
# checked and is reported as such rather than silently passing.
#
# This gate has no grandfather list and no ratchet: it reports every violation
# it finds. Exemptions, if any, are added deliberately as named constants below.

require "prism"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
Dir.chdir(ROOT)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Class index: constant -> superclass, built from source across app/.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PARENT = {}
STRUCT_CONSTS = []
# `Name = T.type_alias { <type> }` — a named interface describing the shape of
# data, not a class. It has no superclass to walk, so it is indexed by its body
# and resolved transitively: the alias is exactly as legal as what it contains.
# `Badge = T.type_alias { { label: String, variant: Symbol } }` is a hash of
# primitives and passes; `PlayerRow = T.type_alias { [ UserPresenter,
# T::Array[Character] ] }` hides raw models one level down and does not.
TYPE_ALIASES = {}

def index_type_alias(node)
  return unless node.is_a?(Prism::ConstantWriteNode)

  value = node.value
  return unless value.is_a?(Prism::CallNode) && value.name == :type_alias
  return if value.block.nil?

  TYPE_ALIASES[node.name.to_s] = value.block.slice
end

def index_classes(node, path, file)
  return if node.nil?

  index_type_alias(node)

  if node.is_a?(Prism::ClassNode)
    name = node.constant_path.slice
    full = path.empty? ? name : "#{path.join('::')}::#{name}"
    superclass = node.superclass&.slice
    PARENT[full] = superclass
    PARENT[name] ||= superclass
    if superclass.to_s.include?("T::Struct")
      STRUCT_CONSTS << full
      STRUCT_CONSTS << name
    end
    node.compact_child_nodes.each { |child| index_classes(child, path + [ name ], file) }
    return
  end

  if node.is_a?(Prism::ModuleNode)
    name = node.constant_path.slice
    node.compact_child_nodes.each { |child| index_classes(child, path + [ name ], file) }
    return
  end

  node.compact_child_nodes.each { |child| index_classes(child, path, file) }
end

Dir["app/**/*.rb"].each do |file|
  index_classes(Prism.parse(File.read(file, encoding: "utf-8")).value, [], file)
end

MODEL_ROOT     = "ApplicationRecord"
PRESENTER_ROOT = "BasePresenter"
COMPONENT_ROOTS = [ "ApplicationComponent", "ViewComponent::Base" ].freeze

# Types that are structural rather than domain objects: rule 4 permits hashes,
# and a T::Struct is a typed hash. Primitives are likewise not a layer.
PRIMITIVES = %w[
  String Symbol Integer Float Numeric TrueClass FalseClass NilClass Object
  Date Time DateTime ActiveSupport::TimeWithZone
].freeze

GENERICS = %w[T T::Array T::Hash T::Boolean T::Set T::Range T::Enumerable].freeze

def ancestry(const)
  chain = [ const ]
  seen = {}
  while (parent = PARENT[chain.last]) && !seen[parent]
    seen[parent] = true
    chain << parent
  end
  chain
end

# :model | :presenter | :component | :struct | :policy | :primitive | :unknown
def classify(const)
  return :primitive if PRIMITIVES.include?(const)
  return :struct if STRUCT_CONSTS.include?(const) ||
    STRUCT_CONSTS.any? { |s| s.split("::").last == const }

  chain = ancestry(const)
  return :model     if chain.include?(MODEL_ROOT)
  return :presenter if chain.include?(PRESENTER_ROOT)
  return :component if COMPONENT_ROOTS.any? { |root| chain.include?(root) } ||
    COMPONENT_ROOTS.include?(const)
  return :policy    if chain.include?("ApplicationPolicy")
  :unknown
end

# The domain constants a type expression exposes to the component, with the
# generic wrappers (T.nilable, T::Array[...]) stripped:
# `T.nilable(T::Array[Post])` -> ["Post"].
#
# A HASH IS OPAQUE. Rule 4 permits hashes, and it permits them without asking
# what is inside: the hash is how a component takes the messy, irregular data a
# real front end needs — html_options, data attributes, a FormBuilder, a config
# bag — without that becoming a typed parameter for every case. Looking inside
# would make the gate an arbiter of the component's own API, which is the
# component's `sig` to declare and Sorbet's to enforce. So `T::Hash[...]` and a
# hash-shaped type alias short-circuit here and expose nothing.
#
# Arrays and tuples are NOT opaque — they are a sequence of the thing itself,
# so `T::Array[Character]` is raw models reaching the component no matter how it
# is named. That is what tells `Badge` (a hash) from `PlayerRow` (a tuple
# holding T::Array[Character]), and stops an alias becoming a place models hide.
HASH_TYPE = /\A(?:T::Hash\[|\{)/

def type_constants(type, seen = [])
  return [] if type.strip.match?(HASH_TYPE)

  type.scan(/[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_:]*/)
    .reject { |const| GENERICS.include?(const) || const == "T" }
    .flat_map do |const|
      short = const.split("::").last
      body = TYPE_ALIASES[const] || TYPE_ALIASES[short]
      if body && !seen.include?(const)
        # An alias naming a hash is a hash: opaque, like any other.
        inner = body.sub(/\A\s*(?:do|\{)\s*/, "").sub(/\s*(?:end|\})\s*\z/, "")
        type_constants(inner.strip.match?(HASH_TYPE) ? "{}" : inner, seen + [ const ])
      else
        [ const ]
      end
    end
    .uniq
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared AST helpers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def each_node(node, &block)
  return if node.nil?
  block.call(node)
  node.compact_child_nodes.each { |child| each_node(child, &block) }
end

def parse(file)
  Prism.parse(File.read(file, encoding: "utf-8")).value
end

# The `sig` preceding `def initialize` — Sorbet's declared types for it.
# Comment lines may sit between the two (`# mutant:disable` does, on five
# components), so they are skipped rather than treated as a missing sig.
SIG_GAP = '(?:\s*#[^\n]*\n)*\s*'

def sig_before_initialize(source)
  source[/sig do(.*?)end#{SIG_GAP}def initialize/m, 1] ||
    source[/sig \{(.*?)\}#{SIG_GAP}def initialize/m, 1]
end

# name => declared type, from a sig's params(...) list.
def sig_params(sig)
  return {} if sig.nil?

  body = sig[/params\((.*)\)\s*\.?\s*(?:void|returns)/m, 1] || sig[/params\((.*)\)/m, 1]
  return {} if body.nil?

  body.scan(/(\w+):\s*(T\.nilable\([^)]*\)|T::Array\[[^\]]*\]|T::Hash\[[^\]]*\]|[A-Z][\w:]*)/).to_h
end

# Every `sig`-annotated method in the file, as method name => {param => type}.
# R4 reads all of them: a component may not accept a model at construction OR
# afterwards, and reading only `initialize` is how five raw-GameMember methods
# hid behind a clean constructor.
def sig_params_by_method(source)
  source.scan(/sig (?:do(.*?)end|\{(.*?)\})#{SIG_GAP}def (\w+)/m).filter_map do |block, brace, method_name|
    params = sig_params(block || brace)
    [ method_name, params ] unless params.empty?
  end
end

def find_initialize(root)
  found = nil
  each_node(root) { |n| found ||= n if n.is_a?(Prism::DefNode) && n.name == :initialize }
  found
end

violations = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R1 — Views hold only presenters.
#
# Read from the controller, not the ERB: a template can only reach a model
# through an ivar, so constraining what a controller assigns constrains what a
# view can hold. `# typed: strict` forces a T.let on every ivar, which is the
# declaration this reads.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Mailers, layouts and error pages are not the view layer this rule governs:
# they have no Pundit and no ViewComponents, and their ivars come from mailer
# methods rather than controller actions.
R1_SKIP_CONTROLLERS = %w[
  application_controller.rb
  data_application_controller.rb
  turnstile_verification.rb
  inbound_emails_controller.rb
].freeze
# inbound_emails_controller.rb: a webhook ingress (Resend -> ActionMailbox),
# not the view layer — it renders no templates, so R1 has nothing to enforce.

# The one ivar a controller may hold that is not view state: RequestMemo's
# store (app/controllers/request_memo.rb). Controller lookups behind
# `before_action` are the controller's working set, not something a template
# should read, but they still need to be memoized — dropping the memo took
# posts#update from 17 queries to 28, because `post` resolves through `scene`
# through `game`. RequestMemo keeps them for the request inside one hash
# instead of beside the view state, so `memo(:game) { Game.find(...) }`
# replaces `@game ||= ...`. This name is exempt; a domain-shaped ivar is not,
# and putting a model in an ivar to dodge the rule is still the violation.
R1_INTERNAL_IVARS = %w[@controller_memo].freeze

def visibility_switch(node)
  return nil unless node.is_a?(Prism::CallNode) && node.receiver.nil?
  return nil unless %i[private protected public].include?(node.name)
  return nil unless node.arguments.nil? || node.arguments.arguments.empty?
  node.name
end

# --- Runtime resolution (optional) -----------------------------------------
#
# When an ivar is assigned from a helper method, the source says nothing about
# what comes back — `@users = players_for_select` could be anything. Sorbet
# already knows: the helper carries a `sig`, and T::Utils.signature_for_method
# reads its declared return type from the loaded object. That turns the largest
# and least useful diagnosis ("check what this returns") into a real answer.
#
# Booting Rails costs ~1.7s against the gate's ~0.1s, so it is opt-out via
# NO_RUNTIME=1 and degrades to source inspection if the app cannot load (a
# broken boot must not turn this gate into a false failure). Everything else in
# this file stays source-based: R3 and R4 ask about the shape of code, not the
# behaviour of objects.
RUNTIME = ENV["NO_RUNTIME"].nil? && begin
  require File.join(ROOT, "config", "environment")
  true
rescue StandardError, LoadError
  false
end

# Declared return type of a controller's helper method, or nil when there is no
# sig to read (~80% of helpers have one).
def helper_return_type(controller_const, method_name)
  return nil unless RUNTIME

  klass = Object.const_get(controller_const)
  return nil unless klass.is_a?(Class)

  method = klass.instance_method(method_name)
  signature = T::Utils.signature_for_method(method)
  return_type = signature&.return_type.to_s
  return nil if return_type.empty? || return_type == "<VOID>"

  return_type
rescue NameError, StandardError
  nil
end

# What an undeclared ivar's assignment says about the work it needs. Two very
# different jobs hide behind "no declared type", and reporting them identically
# makes a file look uniformly broken when half of it is already right:
#   `GamePresenter.new(...)` is CORRECT and only wants the sigil.
#   `Game.find(...)`         is a raw model and wants a presenter written.
# A bare helper call is neither until its return type is resolved, which is what
# the runtime pass is for; without it, such a case is reported as needing a look
# rather than guessed at.
def undeclared_diagnosis(rhs, controller_const = nil)
  if (resolved = resolved_helper_diagnosis(rhs, controller_const))
    return resolved
  end

  case rhs
  when /\A[A-Z][\w:]*Presenter\.new/, /\bmap\b.*[A-Z][\w:]*Presenter\.new/
    "the assignment already looks like a presenter, so this one likely only needs " \
      "the `# typed: strict` sigil and a T.let to become checkable"
  when /\A([A-Z][\w:]*)\.(?:find|new|find_by|where|all|first|last)\b/,
       /\A@?[\w.]*\.(?:find|new|find_by|where|order|includes|all)\b/
    "this assigns a raw model or query result, so it needs a presenter — " \
      "adding the sigil alone will not satisfy R1"
  else
    "check what this returns: a presenter needs only the sigil, a model or " \
      "primitive needs a presenter written for it"
  end
end

# A bare `@x = helper_method` resolved through the helper's declared return
# type, classified the same way any other type expression is.
def resolved_helper_diagnosis(rhs, controller_const)
  return nil if controller_const.nil?
  return nil unless rhs.match?(/\A[a-z_][\w]*\z/) # a bare method call, no receiver

  return_type = helper_return_type(controller_const, rhs.to_sym)
  return nil if return_type.nil?

  kinds = type_constants(return_type).map { |const| classify(const) }.uniq
  if kinds == [ :presenter ]
    "`#{rhs}` returns #{return_type}, already a presenter — this one only needs " \
      "the `# typed: strict` sigil and a T.let to become checkable"
  elsif kinds.include?(:model)
    "`#{rhs}` returns #{return_type}, which carries a raw model — it needs a " \
      "presenter; adding the sigil alone will not satisfy R1"
  else
    "`#{rhs}` returns #{return_type} — views hold presenters, so this needs one " \
      "wrapping it"
  end
end

# Every ivar the controller assigns, with the T.let type when one is declared.
#
# Private methods are included deliberately. Rails controllers set their
# view-facing state from `before_action` callbacks, and those callbacks are
# private — scanning only public actions missed `set_game`/`set_scene`/
# `set_page` entirely, which is where `@game` and `@scene` are assigned in
# nearly every controller. A template can reach a model through such an ivar
# exactly as easily as through one written in the action, so R1 has to see
# both or it reports clean while `@scene.parent_scene.title` sits in an ERB.
#
# A MODULE COUNTS AS A CONTROLLER BODY. The `*Scoped` mixins under
# app/controllers/ hold the `set_game`/`set_scene` lookups for the controllers
# that include them, so scanning only ClassNode left their ivars unchecked —
# `@game` and `@scene` are assigned there and read straight from templates
# (posts/edit.html.erb does exactly this). Extracting a setter into a mixin is
# a fine way to share it, but it must not be a way to stop the rule seeing it.
def controller_ivars(root)
  body = nil
  each_node(root) { |n| body ||= n if n.is_a?(Prism::ClassNode) || n.is_a?(Prism::ModuleNode) }
  return [] unless body

  statements = body.body.is_a?(Prism::StatementsNode) ? body.body.body : []
  visibility = :public
  found = []

  statements.each do |node|
    if (switch = visibility_switch(node))
      visibility = switch
    elsif node.is_a?(Prism::DefNode)
      each_node(node) do |inner|
        next unless inner.is_a?(Prism::InstanceVariableWriteNode) ||
          inner.is_a?(Prism::InstanceVariableOrWriteNode)

        value = inner.value&.slice.to_s
        declared = value[/T\.let\(.*?,\s*(.+)\)\s*\z/m, 1]
        found << { name: inner.name.to_s, action: node.name.to_s, type: declared, rhs: value }
      end
    end
  end

  found
    .reject { |ivar| R1_INTERNAL_IVARS.include?(ivar[:name]) }
    .uniq { |ivar| [ ivar[:name], ivar[:action] ] }
end

Dir["app/controllers/**/*.rb", "app/mailers/**/*.rb"].sort.each do |file|
  next if R1_SKIP_CONTROLLERS.include?(File.basename(file))

  source = File.read(file, encoding: "utf-8")
  strict = source.start_with?("# typed: strict")
  controller_const = file.sub("app/controllers/", "").sub(/\.rb\z/, "").split("/")
    .map { |part| part.split("_").map(&:capitalize).join }.join("::")

  controller_ivars(parse(file)).each do |ivar|
    unless strict
      # Without a T.let there is no declared type to classify, but the
      # assignment itself usually gives the answer away — and the two cases need
      # completely different work. Saying which one this is turns "add a sigil"
      # and "write a presenter that does not exist yet" into distinct tasks
      # instead of one indistinguishable message repeated down the file.
      violations["R1"] << "#{file}: #{ivar[:name]} (in ##{ivar[:action]}) = #{ivar[:rhs]} — " \
        "#{undeclared_diagnosis(ivar[:rhs], controller_const)} (controller is " \
        "`# typed: #{source[/# typed: (\w+)/, 1]}`; only `strict` forces the T.let this rule reads)"
      next
    end

    consts = type_constants(ivar[:type].to_s)
    kinds = consts.map { |const| classify(const) }.uniq

    next if !kinds.empty? && kinds.all? { |kind| kind == :presenter }

    violations["R1"] << "#{file}: #{ivar[:name]} (in ##{ivar[:action]}) is " \
      "#{ivar[:type] || ivar[:rhs]} [#{kinds.join(', ')}] — views hold only presenters"
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R2 — Policies are injected, never looked up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Dir["app/views/**/*.erb", "app/components/**/*.erb"].sort.each do |file|
  File.readlines(file, encoding: "utf-8").each_with_index do |line, index|
    next unless line =~ /\bpolicy\(/
    violations["R2"] << "#{file}:#{index + 1}: calls `policy(...)` — " \
      "inject the policy into the presenter's options hash instead"
  end
end

Dir["app/presenters/**/*.rb", "app/components/**/*.rb"].sort.each do |file|
  each_node(parse(file)) do |node|
    next unless node.is_a?(Prism::CallNode) && node.name == :new
    receiver = node.receiver&.slice.to_s
    next unless receiver.end_with?("Policy")

    violations["R2"] << "#{file}: constructs `#{receiver}.new(...)` — " \
      "a presenter/component is handed a policy, it does not build one"
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R3 — Presenters take (subject, options).
#
# Structural: exactly one required positional and a **options rest, with no
# loose keywords. The subject's own type is deliberately not inspected — a
# model, a presenter, or an array of either are all legitimate subjects.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Dir["app/presenters/**/*.rb"].sort.each do |file|
  root = parse(file)
  klass = nil
  each_node(root) { |n| klass ||= n if n.is_a?(Prism::ClassNode) }
  next if klass.nil?
  # Only presenters proper: BasePresenter itself defines the contract, and a
  # T::Struct value object living here is not a presenter.
  next unless classify(klass.constant_path.slice) == :presenter
  next if File.basename(file) == "base_presenter.rb"

  init = find_initialize(root)
  next if init.nil? # inherits BasePresenter's conforming (model, **options)

  params = init.parameters
  positionals = params&.requireds&.size.to_i
  optionals = params&.optionals&.size.to_i
  keywords = params&.keywords&.size.to_i
  kwrest = params&.keyword_rest
  rest = params&.rest

  problems = []
  problems << "#{positionals} positional parameters (expected exactly 1: the subject)" if positionals != 1
  problems << "#{optionals} optional positional parameter(s)" if optionals.positive?
  problems << "#{keywords} loose keyword parameter(s) (collaborators belong in the options hash)" if keywords.positive?
  problems << "a splat parameter" unless rest.nil?
  problems << "no **options parameter" if kwrest.nil?

  next if problems.empty?

  violations["R3"] << "#{file}: `initialize(#{params&.slice})` — #{problems.join('; ')}"
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R4 — Components take presenters, components, arrays of components, hashes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Dir["app/components/**/*.rb"].sort.each do |file|
  source = File.read(file, encoding: "utf-8")
  root = parse(file)
  klass = nil
  each_node(root) { |n| klass ||= n if n.is_a?(Prism::ClassNode) }
  next if klass.nil?
  next unless classify(klass.constant_path.slice) == :component

  init = find_initialize(root)
  next if init.nil?

  if sig_before_initialize(source).nil?
    violations["R4"] << "#{file}: `initialize` has no sig — parameter types cannot be checked"
    next
  end

  # EVERY method, not just initialize. Shared::NavDrawerComponent took a clean
  # UserPresenter at the door and raw GameMember rows in five methods below it,
  # and R4 never saw them because it read one signature. A component that
  # accepts a model after construction is holding a model just the same.
  sig_params_by_method(source).each do |method_name, params|
    params.each do |name, type|
    consts = type_constants(type)
    next if consts.empty?

    kinds = consts.map { |const| classify(const) }.uniq
    # Presenters, components and primitives are the leaves; hashes, structs and
    # type aliases are permitted CONTAINERS, already resolved to their contents
    # by type_constants. A container holding a model still fails — that is the
    # PlayerRow case, and the reason containers are not simply whitelisted.
    next if (kinds - [ :presenter, :component, :struct, :primitive ]).empty?

    label = method_name == "initialize" ? "`#{name}: #{type}`" : "##{method_name}'s `#{name}: #{type}`"
    violations["R4"] << "#{file}: #{label} [#{kinds.join(', ')}] — " \
      "components take presenters, components, arrays of components, hashes and structs"
    end
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Report
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DESCRIPTIONS = {
  "R1" => "Views hold only presenters",
  "R2" => "Policies are injected, never looked up",
  "R3" => "Presenters take (subject, options)",
  "R4" => "Components take presenters, components, arrays of components, hashes"
}.freeze

total = violations.values.sum(&:size)

if total.zero?
  puts "View-layering check: OK (all four layering rules hold)"
  exit 0
end

puts "View-layering check: FAIL — #{total} violation(s)\n\n"

%w[R1 R2 R3 R4].each do |rule|
  found = violations[rule]
  puts "#{rule} — #{DESCRIPTIONS[rule]}: #{found.size} violation(s)"
  found.each { |line| puts "    #{line}" }
  puts
end

puts <<~GUIDANCE
  ============================================================================
  WHAT THIS MEANS AND WHAT TO DO NEXT
  ============================================================================

  Each violation above says the same thing: a decision is being made in the
  wrong layer. Move that decision to the layer that owns it. The resulting code
  is usually smaller than what it replaces — applying this to
  ScenePresenter#page_action took it from five parameters to three and dropped
  the file's smell count — even though the diff is wide, because a signature
  change touches every caller. Expect fewer lines across more files.

  You are probably here mid-task, wanting the smallest change that gets you
  moving again. The four shortcuts below all look like that change, and each
  leaves the codebase worse than the violation did:

    Weakening the gate — an exemption, a skip list, a ratchet, a `next if` for
    your file. This gate reports every violation deliberately, so that a leak
    is visible while someone is in a position to fix it. Silencing it converts
    a fixable problem into an invisible one.

    Moving the violation — putting the model in a hash, renaming the
    parameter, wrapping it in a struct, hoisting it into the controller. The
    model still reaches the template; it is just harder to find next time.

    Passing a model "just this once" because the presenter does not exist yet.
    Writing that presenter IS the task the violation is describing.

    Reading a rule as a naming convention. Classification is by inheritance:
    calling a class FooPresenter does not make it one.

  Believe a violation is wrong? Say so to the user — name the file, the type,
  and your reasoning — rather than working around it or editing this file.

  A clean refactor touches every call site, and that is fine.
  Fixing one of these usually changes a constructor signature, which means
  visiting everywhere it is constructed — controllers, views, specs, previews.
  A long list of touched files here is the shape of the work, not evidence you
  chose the wrong approach.

  The tempting alternative is to keep the old shape working: leave the old
  parameter in place, give the new collaborator a default, migrate the happy
  path and leave the error branch. Each of those leaves the object with two
  valid ways to be constructed, and nothing marks which one is current. The
  next person to read it has to reconstruct the intent from call sites that
  disagree — a worse problem than the violation, and one no gate detects.
  Prefer changing the signature, updating every caller, and deleting the old
  path. If that is more than you can finish, fix fewer violations completely
  rather than leaving a half-migration behind.

  Expect R1 to rise when you fix R2 or R3.
  Injecting a policy usually means a new presenter, and a new presenter means a
  new controller instance variable. If that controller is not yet
  `# typed: strict`, the new ivar is reported under R1 immediately — so the
  total can go up while the codebase gets better. That is expected, and not a
  regression to undo. Judge the work by the violations you actually resolved,
  not by the headline number.

  ============================================================================
  THE ARCHITECTURE THIS GATE ENFORCES
  ============================================================================

  THE FOUR LAYERS
  ---------------
    MODEL      Domain state and behaviour. Knows nothing about being shown.
    PRESENTER  Display LOGIC. Turns a model into presentation-ready values:
               formatted dates, derived flags, capability answers, labels.
               Decides WHAT is shown. Never emits markup.
    COMPONENT  Markup. Decides HOW something is shown: HTML structure, CSS
               classes, which sub-components render. Never computes what to
               show from a model.
    VIEW       Composition only. Renders components and reads presenters.
               Holds no logic of its own.

  The split that matters most, because most violations blur it:
  presenters manage display logic; components choose markup. A presenter
  saying "this scene shows a Private badge, yellow" is display logic and
  belongs in the presenter. The component turning that into a
  Ui::BadgeComponent is markup and belongs in the component. If a component
  is deciding whether something is private, the presenter is missing.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R1 — VIEWS HOLD ONLY PRESENTERS
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every instance variable a view touches must be a presenter. Not a model, not
  a component, not a primitive, not an array of models.

  WHY: a raw model in a template can be asked anything — `@game.scenes.where(...)`
  is available right there — so "no logic in ERB" is unenforceable at the
  boundary. The data being present is the problem. A presenter exposes a
  finished, named answer instead of an object to interrogate.

  Nor a component: assigning a controller-built component would
  satisfy "no raw model in the ERB" while moving the model into the controller.
  The controller hands the view ONE kind of thing; components are constructed
  in templates from presenter data.

  HOW TO FIX — three shapes, by what the ivar currently holds:

    A MODEL -> wrap it, expose what the view needs as named methods:
      # controller
      @scene_presenter = T.let(ScenePresenter.new(scene, policy: ...),
                               ScenePresenter)
      # view
      <%= @scene_presenter.status_label %>

    A PRIMITIVE (a boolean, a count, an id list) -> this is a presenter method
    that got inlined into the controller. The controller computed it because
    the view needed an answer; that answer is display logic. Move the
    computation onto the presenter and delete the ivar.

    A COLLECTION -> either an array of presenters, or a presenter that presents
    the collection. Prefer the latter when the view asks anything ABOUT the
    collection — how many, whether it is empty, what order it is in — because
    those questions otherwise become logic in the template.

  When you add a presenter, two other checks in this repo apply to it: register
  the class in .mutant.yml under matcher.subjects (the mutant_registration job
  fails otherwise), and give the file a `# typed: strict` sigil like its
  neighbours in app/presenters.

  NOTE: this rule reads the `T.let` type Sorbet requires under `# typed:
  strict`. A controller reported as "no declared type" is not yet strict —
  make it strict (`srb tc -a` autocorrects most declarations) so its ivars
  become checkable.

  Two different jobs hide behind "no declared type", and each message above
  says which one it is. Do not treat a file as uniformly broken:
    "already looks like a presenter" — the assignment is right; it only needs
      the sigil and a T.let. Cheap. A controller can be mostly in this state.
    "assigns a raw model or query result" — a presenter has to be written
      first. Adding the sigil alone will NOT satisfy R1, it will just change
      the message to a typed violation.
    "`helper` returns X" — the assignment called a helper, and X is that
      helper's declared return type, read off the loaded app. Treat it as the
      ivar's real type: a presenter needs only the sigil, anything carrying a
      model needs one written.
    "check what this returns" — an inline expression with no declared type to
      read, so open it and see which of the two cases above it is.
  A single controller usually contains both kinds. Doing the sigil-only ones
  does not fix the model ones, and a `T.let(..., Game)` that makes the parser
  happy while a raw model still reaches the view is the violation unchanged.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R2 — POLICIES ARE INJECTED, NEVER LOOKED UP
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A view never calls `policy(...)`. A presenter or component never calls
  `SomePolicy.new`. Policy instances are handed to the presenter at
  construction, in the options hash: `{ policy: <instance> }`.

  A capability is what is being decided — "may this user administer this game",
  "may this user resolve this scene" — named for the function, not the person.
  A role is who the person is (`gm?`, `owner?`). The distinction matters because
  a GM is the game owner today and will not always be: a game may have several
  GMs, or none, or permissions may granularize. Writing the capability as the
  role bakes "owner == GM == can do everything" into every call site, so the
  rule can never change in one place.

  Why: authorization reached-for rather than supplied means a capability rename
  has to be chased through templates instead of changed at one construction
  point. Ask a capability (`manage?`, `resolve?`), never a role (`gm?`) and
  never a CRUD predicate borrowed for a different purpose (`update?` means
  "this row may be modified", not "is GM").

  Why it pays for itself in specs: a presenter spec must never test a policy —
  policies are tested in spec/policies. Today a spec has to stub the constant
  AND know how the presenter builds it internally:
      allow(PostPolicy).to receive(:new).with(user, new_post)
        .and_return(instance_double(PostPolicy, create?: create))
  That `.with(...)` is private implementation leaking into the test. Injected,
  it is one line with no constant reference:
      described_class.new(scene, policy: instance_double(PostPolicy, create?: true))

  HOW TO FIX
    Controller (where the user and record are already in hand). Use Pundit's
    own `policy(...)` helper rather than constructing the policy class
    directly — it is memoized and it is what the rest of the controller
    already authorizes with, so the affordance and the authorization cannot
    diverge. R2 forbids policy LOOKUP in views, presenters and components;
    a controller is exactly where it belongs:
      @game_presenter = T.let(
        GamePresenter.new(game, policy: policy(game)),
        GamePresenter
      )
    Presenter:
      sig { returns(T::Boolean) }
      def can_manage? = @options.fetch(:policy).manage?
    View: `<% if @game_presenter.can_manage? %>` — no policy call anywhere.

    Use `fetch` with no default. A missing collaborator should raise at the
    call site that forgot it, not silently answer false and hide a broken
    affordance.

    Naming the option: a presenter needing one policy uses `policy:`. When it
    needs more than one, name each for its subject — `game_policy:`,
    `character_policy:`, `post_policy:` — so the call site says which record
    is being authorized rather than leaving it to be inferred.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R3 — PRESENTERS TAKE (subject, options)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One positional subject — a MODEL, another PRESENTER, or an ARRAY of either —
  plus an options hash. Nothing else: no second positional, no loose keyword
  arguments, no url_helpers, no view context.

  WHY: a free-form signature is how
  `page_action(game:, can_manage:, is_participant:, membership:, urls:)`
  happened — five parameters, one of which handed the presenter a CAPABILITY
  (`urls: self`) instead of finished data. Collaborators travel in the options
  hash so the contract lives in BasePresenter rather than being reinvented per
  subclass.

  A presenter subject is legal and intended. Presenters compose: a presenter
  wrapping a presenter, or presenting an array of presenters, is how collection
  display logic ("how many", "empty state", ordering) gets out of the template.

  HOW TO FIX
      # before
      def initialize(model, current_user)
      def initialize(model, scene_participants: [])
      # after
      def initialize(model, **options)   # BasePresenter already does this
        super
      end
    Read collaborators from @options. Applying this rule makes code SMALLER:
    ScenePresenter#page_action went 5 params -> 3 and its reek count dropped.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R4 — COMPONENTS TAKE PRESENTERS, COMPONENTS, ARRAYS OF COMPONENTS, HASHES
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Never a raw model.

  WHY: a component holding a model computes what the presenter should have —
  that is where presenter/component split violations start.

  What the classifier does, so you can predict it:
    * Classification is BY INHERITANCE, NOT BY NAME. Every class under app/ is
      indexed with its superclass and resolved by walking to ApplicationRecord
      (model) / BasePresenter (presenter) / ApplicationComponent (component).
      `SceneSummary` (model) and `SceneSummaryPresenter` (presenter) sort
      correctly, and a `class FooPresenter` that never inherits BasePresenter
      is NOT a presenter no matter what it is called.
    * A hash is opaque. The gate permits hashes and does not look inside. The
      hash is how a component takes the messy, irregular reality of a front end
      — html_options, data attributes, a form binding, a config bag — without
      each case becoming a typed parameter. This localizes the mess rather than
      blessing it. What is in the hash is the component's own `sig` to declare
      and Sorbet's to enforce, not this gate's.

      So `T::Hash[Symbol, SomeModel]` PASSES this check. That is a limit of what
      the gate can see, not permission — it will fail review instead. Keep the
      two apart: "the check is silent" and "this is right" are different claims,
      and only one of them is mechanical. The test for whether a hash is honest:
      would you have written it this way if the gate did look inside? A hash
      that exists to carry options, attributes or configuration is what the rule
      intends. A hash whose keys are model ids, or that pairs with an array of
      the models it indexes, is a join a presenter should have done — you have
      found a second violation, not a way around the first.
    * Arrays, tuples and type aliases are transparent — resolved through to
      their contents. An array is a sequence of the thing itself, so
      `T::Array[Character]` is raw models entering the component however it is
      named. This is why `Badge = T.type_alias { { label: String, variant:
      Symbol } }` passes (a hash) and `PlayerRow = T.type_alias { [
      UserPresenter, T::Array[Character] ] }` fails (a tuple hiding models).
      `T::Array[SomePresenter]` is fine — transparent, and presenters are legal.
      So is an array of primitives; only models are rejected.

  CONTAINER SHAPES — which to reach for:
    T.type_alias  A NAMED INTERFACE for data written as a LITERAL at the call
                  site. No constructor. Use when the shape is just data.
    T::Struct     The same data when something CONSTRUCTS it. Gets defaults,
                  validation and dot-access. Use when there is a `.new`.
    Presenter     When the shape pairs display data with domain objects, or has
                  behaviour. If a "shape" holds a model, this is your answer.
    The choice is about WHO BUILDS THE THING, not about layering — all three are
    containers to this gate, and all are exactly as legal as their contents.

  HOW TO FIX
      # before — the component receives models and works out what to show
      def initialize(game:, scene:, posts:)
      # after — the presenter carries the finished values
      def initialize(scene_presenter:)

    Ask what the component actually reads off each model. If it reads two or
    three values, the presenter exposes those values. If it reads a value
    per item in a collection, give the ITEM its own presenter and pass an
    array of them — do not pass models plus a lookup table keyed by id.

    When the component genuinely needs a domain object to render a link or a
    form binding, it needs the value it would derive from it (a path, a name,
    an id), not the object. Derive it in the presenter and pass the result.

  A hash is not a loophole
    Because the gate cannot see inside a hash, moving a model into one silences
    the check without changing anything — the model still reaches the component.
    A worked example of the smell: `editor_names: T::Hash[Integer, String]`
    passed alongside `versions: T::Array[CharacterVersion]` is a lookup table
    keyed by model id, which is a join a CharacterVersionPresenter should have
    done by exposing `editor_name` per version. The array is the R4 violation;
    the hash is the evidence a presenter is missing.

  ============================================================================
  HOW TO COMPOSE, END TO END
  ============================================================================
    1. CONTROLLER builds presenters, injecting policies and collaborators:
         @scene_presenter = T.let(
           ScenePresenter.new(scene, policy: ScenePolicy.new(current_user, scene),
                                     game: game),
           ScenePresenter
         )
    2. PRESENTER answers display questions as named methods — capabilities from
       the injected policy, formatted values, derived flags, collections of
       presenters.
    3. VIEW composes: renders components, passing presenter values.
    4. COMPONENT owns markup and CSS, receiving finished data — presenters,
       other components, arrays of components, hashes.

  THE TEST TO APPLY TO YOUR FIX
    Does the model still reach the template, by any route (ivar, component
    parameter, array, tuple, or hidden in a hash)? If yes, the violation moved
    instead of being fixed. The decision belongs one layer down, every time.

    Then: is there exactly one way to construct what you changed, and does
    every call site use it? A compatibility shim, a defaulted collaborator or
    an un-migrated branch leaves the codebase with two shapes for one object,
    which is worse than the violation you started with.

  This gate has no grandfather list and no ratchet: every violation is
  reported. Adding an exemption to make it pass defeats its purpose.

  To re-run after a fix:  bin/check-view-layering
  It also runs in bin/pre-push and as its own CI job. A new presenter needs
  registering in .mutant.yml, and `bundle exec srb tc` must stay clean.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This file is the enforced statement of these rules — the rules and the code
  that checks them live together here so they cannot drift apart. Other
  documents (.claude/CLAUDE.md) point at this gate rather than restating it.
  Everything needed to fix a violation is in this output; there is nothing
  further to read.
GUIDANCE

exit 1
